WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.631
One resource, six endpoints. The address names the thing. The method says what to do to it.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:19.762
A task list needs create, delete, update. So the beginner writes /createTask, and /deleteTask?id=6.

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:25.361
Every action gets its own address, and every API invents different ones.

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:31.857
REST turns it round: one address per thing, and the HTTP method is the action.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:41.611
Our thing is tasks. A GET request to /api/tasks lists them. 200: five tasks.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:45.957
One plural noun for the collection. Never a verb.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:52.995
To add one, POST to the same address, with a title in the body: Study REST.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:01:01.647
201 Created. The Location header gives the new address: /api/tasks/6.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.747
That address is the task. A GET to it answers 200, with the task.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:14.925
A GET changes nothing, so asking twice is harmless. That's what safe means.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:22.752
To tick it off, send a PATCH to the same address, with only the field that changes: done: true.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.358
200 again. Every other field stays as it was.

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:34.371
A PUT would replace the whole task, so it has to send every field. A PATCH changes just one.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:42.335
DELETE the same address. 204 No Content. Nothing to send back.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:49.864
Ask for it once more. 404. The address never changed. Only the task is gone.

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:55.565
The client knew what happened from the number alone, before it read a single byte of body.

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:05.006
Now a train enters a tunnel, and the answer is lost. Did the server act? The app can only send it again.

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:14.451
Send DELETE twice: the second answers 404, but the state is identical. The task is gone either way. Same for PUT.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:18.980
Send POST twice, and there's a duplicate: task 7.

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:26.775
The cure is an Idempotency-Key header. Same key on the retry, and the server repeats its first answer.

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:35.963
Look at what the story wrote. One address for the collection, one for the item, and a method and a status for each line.

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:41.626
Add PUT, which replaces the whole task, and that's the full set of six.

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:48.067
Nouns in the address. Verbs in the method. The story in the status code.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:54.050
Practise it with the API request builder in Designing APIs.
